<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerald Durrell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1925–1995</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerald Durrell was a British naturalist, writer and conservationist. He led animal-collecting expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, British Guiana and elsewhere and described them in popular books such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bafut-beagles">The Bafut Beagles</a>, while My Family and Other Animals told of his childhood on Corfu. In 1959 he founded Jersey Zoo, which pioneered the captive breeding of endangered species.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerald-durrell.jpg" alt="Gerald Durrell" width="249" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Byron Patchett · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerald_Durrell,_Askania_Nova_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
